<script>
  import Screenshot from '$lib/components/Screenshot.svelte';
</script>

From the square crop to the selfie and now the vertical video, social media sites like Instagram, Snapchat and Tiktok have had a huge influence on how the world consumes photographs and video.

They've done more than change the way we make images. They've changed the way we use them to tell stories.

One straightforward example is the photo essay. While photo galleries are far from new — Life magazine was publishing them in the 1930s — the format has evolved online.

The photo essays we see on social media are designed for phones, with one image filling the screen at a time, and navigation that encourages tapping through. They're not just a collection of images — they're a sequence. When they're well done, they're also a story.

News organizations have taken a page from social media's playbook and designed story formats that emulate social media designs.

One example is ["Forty Hours Later, a Shot at a Vaccine,"](https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2021/02/03/multimedia/virus-vaccine-kentucky.html) a 2021 photo essay from The New York Times that documented the early COVID vaccine rollout in Kentucky.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-10/nyt-kentucky-vaccine.png"
  alt="The New York Times photo essay on the COVID vaccine rollout in Kentucky, showing a full-screen photograph with a caption overlay at the bottom and dot navigation indicators"
  chromeTitle="Vaccine in Kentucky"
  chromeUrl="https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2021/02/03/multimedia/virus-vaccine-kentucky.html"
/>

It's just one example of how news websites and apps are experimenting with new formats that borrow from social media.

When done well, what distinguishes these stories from amateur creators is the craft and care that goes into them. Unlike the "photo dumps" of social media, professional galleries arrange high-quality images in a deliberate sequence.

Today you will see how professionals build these stories, and then you'll build your own. Our demonstration case will be a set of photographs taken by a famous photojournalist whose work has entered the public domain.

## Part 1: Introducing Gordon Parks

[Gordon Parks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gordon_Parks) (1912–2006) was the first Black photographer hired by the Farm Security Administration, the federal agency that documented American life during the Great Depression and World War II. He called his camera "a weapon against poverty and racism."

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-10/gordon-parks-foundation.png"
  alt="The Gordon Parks Foundation website showing a black-and-white portrait of Gordon Parks and information about his life and legacy"
  chromeTitle="The Gordon Parks Foundation"
  chromeUrl="https://www.gordonparksfoundation.org"
/>

In the spring and summer of 1943, Parks photographed daily life in Harlem for the Office of War Information. He shot portraits of residents, newsboys, street scenes, children, families, and the social fabric of the neighborhood. The images are intimate, dignified, and alive.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-10/loc-fsa-collection.png"
  alt="The Library of Congress FSA/OWI Black-and-White Negatives collection page showing featured photographs by Gordon Parks, Dorothea Lange, Walker Evans, and other photographers"
  chromeTitle="Library of Congress"
  chromeUrl="https://www.loc.gov/collections/fsa-owi-black-and-white-negatives/"
/>

Because the work was produced for the federal government, every image is in the public domain. The Library of Congress holds the original negatives and offers high-resolution scans, free to download, free to publish, free to use for any purpose.

We're going to curate a selection of these photographs and build them into a visual story. But we're not going to make a traditional web page with text paragraphs and images stacked in a column. We're going to make something that feels native to a phone.

### Set up your project

Open your browser and navigate to our template repository at <a href="https://github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template" target="_blank">github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template</a>.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/template-repo-homepage.png"
  alt="GitHub template repository page showing the Use this template button"
  chromeTitle="cuny-jour-static-site-template"
  chromeUrl="https://github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template"
/>

Click "Use this template" and create a new repository called `parks-harlem-1943`. Clone it, open it in VS Code, and run:

```bash
npm install
```

Then:

```bash
npm run dev
```

The photographs are already in the repo. When you cloned it, a set of Gordon Parks images curated from the Library of Congress came with it — you'll find them at `static/photos/`:

```
static/
  photos/
    parks-harlem-01.jpg
    parks-harlem-02.jpg
    parks-harlem-03.jpg
    parks-harlem-04.jpg
    gordon-parks-portrait.jpg
```

We'll build the data file that describes them a little later, once we have something on screen to see.

## Part 2: The component library

Let's take a look at the tools we'll use before we start building.

Just like last week, we're going to lean on the pre-built components in the [class template's Storybook](https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/). Open it now and look at what's available for this project.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-10/storybook-slideshow-components.png"
  alt="The Storybook showing the slideshow components"
  chromeTitle="Storybook"
  chromeUrl="https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

You'll see five components grouped under "Slideshow":

| Component | Description |
|-----------|-------------|
| [`SlideGallery`](https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/?path=/docs/components-slidegallery--docs) | The container that manages navigation, dots, and the horizontal slide track |
| [`TitleSlide`](https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/?path=/docs/components-titleslide--docs) | The opening card with headline, intro, and byline |
| [`PhotoSlide`](https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/?path=/docs/components-photoslide--docs) | A full-screen image with a caption overlay |
| [`TextSlide`](https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/?path=/docs/components-textslide--docs) | A full-screen text card for chapter breaks |
| [`CreditsSlide`](https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/?path=/docs/components-creditsslide--docs) | The closing card for attribution and methodology |

Click through each one in Storybook. Use the controls panel to experiment with the props. This is exactly how you'd explore a component library at a real newsroom — understand what each piece does before you start assembling.

### Getting something on screen

Now open `src/routes/+page.svelte`, clear the boilerplate, and put the first two components to work. No data yet — just wire up `SlideGallery` and `TitleSlide` directly with the headline and intro we want:

```svelte
<script>
  import SlideGallery from '$lib/components/SlideGallery.svelte';
  import TitleSlide from '$lib/components/TitleSlide.svelte';
</script>

<SlideGallery>
  <TitleSlide
    headline="Harlem, 1943"
    intro="In the spring of 1943, Gordon Parks walked Harlem's streets with a government camera and a personal mission: to show what official photographers had long ignored."
    byline="Photographs by Gordon Parks · Farm Security Administration–Office of War Information"
  />
</SlideGallery>
```

Save and check your browser. The full-screen title card should already be there — masthead at the top, headline centered, "Tap to begin →" at the bottom.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-10/title-slide.png"
  alt="A black screen showing the NYCity News Service masthead at top, the headline 'Harlem, 1943' centered, and a 'Tap to begin' prompt at the bottom"
  chromeTitle="Parks Harlem 1943"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="400px"
/>

That's the whole shell of the story. One component, four props, done. Now we need to fill it with photographs.

## Part 3: The beginning

Every story needs a hook. Let's add the opening photographs — the images that made us want to tell this story in the first place.

For us, that's the newsboys. Parks kept returning to them. There's something in his attention to these kids — their dignity, their presence on the street — that sets the tone for the whole essay.

Create `src/lib/data/photos.json` and start with just these two entries:

```json
[
  {
    "id": 1,
    "type": "photo",
    "filename": "parks-harlem-01.jpg",
    "title": "Harlem newsboy",
    "date": "April 1943",
    "caption": "A young newsboy on the streets of Harlem. Parks photographed the children of the neighborhood with a warmth that conveyed their individuality and dignity.",
    "credit": "Gordon Parks / FSA–OWI / Library of Congress"
  },
  {
    "id": 2,
    "type": "photo",
    "filename": "parks-harlem-02.jpg",
    "title": "A Harlem newsboy",
    "date": "May 1943",
    "caption": "Parks spent weeks on Harlem's streets, returning again and again to the same corners, the same faces.",
    "credit": "Gordon Parks / FSA–OWI / Library of Congress"
  }
]
```

You'll fill this file out gradually throughout the class — starting with the opening, then the middle, then the end. The story will take shape in the same order you'd think about it editorially.

Now create `src/routes/+page.js` to load it, following the same pattern from [Week 9](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-9/):

```js
import photos from '$lib/data/photos.json';

export function load() {
  return {
    photos,
  };
}
```

Then update `+page.svelte` to receive the data, import `PhotoSlide`, and loop through the entries:

```svelte
<script>
  import SlideGallery from '$lib/components/SlideGallery.svelte';
  import TitleSlide from '$lib/components/TitleSlide.svelte';
  import PhotoSlide from '$lib/components/PhotoSlide.svelte';

  let { data } = $props();
</script>

<SlideGallery>
  <TitleSlide
    headline="Harlem, 1943"
    intro="In the spring of 1943, Gordon Parks walked Harlem's streets with a government camera and a personal mission: to show what official photographers had long ignored."
    byline="Photographs by Gordon Parks · Farm Security Administration–Office of War Information"
  />

  {#each data.photos as slide}
    {#if slide.type === 'photo'}
      <PhotoSlide photo={slide} />
    {/if}
  {/each}
</SlideGallery>
```

Save and tap through. The title card opens the gallery, followed by the two newsboy photographs.

Three slides, a clear opening. That's your lede.

## Part 4: The middle

A gallery of photographs without context is just a slideshow. What turns it into a story is what you put between the images — the words that explain what the reader is looking at and why it matters.

Open `photos.json` and add the middle of the story: two text cards that give readers the context they need, with more photographs between and after them.

```json
[
  {
    "id": 1,
    "type": "photo",
    "filename": "parks-harlem-01.jpg",
    "title": "Harlem newsboy",
    "date": "April 1943",
    "caption": "A young newsboy on the streets of Harlem. Parks photographed the children of the neighborhood with a warmth that conveyed their individuality and dignity.",
    "credit": "Gordon Parks / FSA–OWI / Library of Congress"
  },
  {
    "id": 2,
    "type": "photo",
    "filename": "parks-harlem-02.jpg",
    "title": "A Harlem newsboy",
    "date": "May 1943",
    "caption": "Parks spent weeks on Harlem's streets, returning again and again to the same corners, the same faces.",
    "credit": "Gordon Parks / FSA–OWI / Library of Congress"
  },
  {
    "id": 3,
    "type": "text",
    "headline": "THE ASSIGNMENT",
    "body": "Parks was the first Black photographer hired by the Farm Security Administration. Sent to Harlem, he found a neighborhood the federal government had mostly documented as a problem. He saw something else entirely."
  },
  {
    "id": 4,
    "type": "photo",
    "filename": "parks-harlem-03.jpg",
    "title": "Three boys in the Harlem area",
    "date": "May–June 1943",
    "caption": "Parks found his subjects in doorways, on stoops, at the edge of the frame — wherever the light fell and the moment was right.",
    "credit": "Gordon Parks / FSA–OWI / Library of Congress"
  },
  {
    "id": 5,
    "type": "text",
    "headline": "THE KIDS",
    "body": "Parks lingered on the children of Harlem — newsboys, corner kids working the same streets their parents did. He photographed them not as symbols of poverty but as individuals with faces worth remembering."
  },
  {
    "id": 6,
    "type": "photo",
    "filename": "parks-harlem-04.jpg",
    "title": "A Harlem newsboy",
    "date": "May 1943",
    "caption": "The newsboys of Harlem worked before and after school, hawking papers on corners throughout the neighborhood.",
    "credit": "Gordon Parks / FSA–OWI / Library of Congress"
  }
]
```

Now update `+page.svelte` to handle the new `"text"` type by adding a `TextSlide` import and an `{:else if}` branch:

```svelte
<script>
  import SlideGallery from '$lib/components/SlideGallery.svelte';
  import TitleSlide from '$lib/components/TitleSlide.svelte';
  import PhotoSlide from '$lib/components/PhotoSlide.svelte';
  import TextSlide from '$lib/components/TextSlide.svelte';

  let { data } = $props();
</script>

<SlideGallery>
  <TitleSlide
    headline="Harlem, 1943"
    intro="In the spring of 1943, Gordon Parks walked Harlem's streets with a government camera and a personal mission: to show what official photographers had long ignored."
    byline="Photographs by Gordon Parks · Farm Security Administration–Office of War Information"
  />

  {#each data.photos as slide}
    {#if slide.type === 'photo'}
      <PhotoSlide photo={slide} />
    {:else if slide.type === 'text'}
      <TextSlide {slide} />
    {/if}
  {/each}
</SlideGallery>
```

Save and tap through. The text cards break the sequence into chapters, giving readers a moment to breathe and orient before the next set of images.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-10/text-slide.png"
  alt="A full-screen black card with centered white text showing 'THE ASSIGNMENT' as a chapter heading and a paragraph of context beneath it"
  chromeTitle="Parks Harlem 1943"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="400px"
/>

Notice that the Svelte code didn't change much — just one new import and one new `{:else if}` branch. All the editorial decisions about sequence, pacing, and what text appears where live in `photos.json`. If you want to reorder slides, add another chapter break, or change the caption on a photograph, you edit the data file. The code stays the same.

## Part 5: The end

Every story needs a conclusion. Ours closes with two things: a portrait of the photographer himself — a reminder of who made these images and what became of him — and a credits slide that tells readers where to find the original work.

Open `photos.json` one more time and add the final two entries:

```json
  {
    "id": 7,
    "type": "photo",
    "filename": "gordon-parks-portrait.jpg",
    "title": "Gordon Parks, 1912–2006",
    "date": "",
    "caption": "Parks went on to become one of the most celebrated photographers of the twentieth century — shooting for Life, covering the civil rights movement, and directing Hollywood films. In 2014, MoMA acquired prints from this Harlem series for its permanent collection.",
    "credit": "[Credit per Wikimedia Commons license]"
  }
```

The portrait is just another `"photo"` entry. No special handling needed — the loop renders it exactly like any other image. But its position at the end, its subject, and its caption give the story a different kind of weight than the street photographs. That's an editorial choice, not a technical one.

Now add the `CreditsSlide` to close the whole thing out. It uses the `children` pattern from [Week 3](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-3/#part-3-creating-a-parent-component), so you write the content between its opening and closing tags:

```svelte
<script>
  import SlideGallery from '$lib/components/SlideGallery.svelte';
  import TitleSlide from '$lib/components/TitleSlide.svelte';
  import PhotoSlide from '$lib/components/PhotoSlide.svelte';
  import TextSlide from '$lib/components/TextSlide.svelte';
  import CreditsSlide from '$lib/components/CreditsSlide.svelte';

  let { data } = $props();
</script>

<SlideGallery>
  <TitleSlide
    headline="Harlem, 1943"
    intro="In the spring of 1943, Gordon Parks walked Harlem's streets with a government camera and a personal mission: to show what official photographers had long ignored."
    byline="Photographs by Gordon Parks · Farm Security Administration–Office of War Information"
  />

  {#each data.photos as slide}
    {#if slide.type === 'photo'}
      <PhotoSlide photo={slide} />
    {:else if slide.type === 'text'}
      <TextSlide {slide} />
    {/if}
  {/each}

  <CreditsSlide>
    <h2>About These Photographs</h2>
    <p>
      The Harlem negatives are held by the Library of Congress and are free to
      download and reuse. High-resolution scans are available at the
      <a href="https://www.loc.gov/pictures/search/?q=gordon+parks+harlem&co=fsa" target="_blank">
        Library of Congress Prints &amp; Photographs Division</a>.
    </p>
  </CreditsSlide>
</SlideGallery>
```

Save and tap all the way through. You now have a complete story with a beginning, a middle, and an end.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-10/credits-slide.png"
