  chromeTitle="Parks Harlem 1943"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="400px"
/>

## Part 6: Testing on your phone

We've been building on a desktop browser, but the whole point of this design is mobile. Let's see how it actually feels on a phone.

SvelteKit's development server is already running on your computer. Your phone can access it if both devices are on the same WiFi network.

Find your computer's local IP address. On Mac, open System Settings, click Network, and look for your IP — it will be something like `192.168.1.42`. On Windows, open a command prompt and type `ipconfig`.

On your phone's browser, navigate to `http://192.168.1.42:5173`, replacing the IP with your own.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-10/phone-preview.png"
  alt="A hand holding a smartphone displaying the Gordon Parks photo gallery with a full-screen portrait photograph and caption overlay at the bottom"
  chromeTitle="Phone Preview"
  chromeUrl="http://192.168.1.42:5173"
  maxWidth="400px"
/>

Tap through the gallery. Each slide should snap into place. The dots at the bottom should track your position. The captions should be readable against the gradient.

This is the experience we designed for. On a phone it doesn't feel like a website. It feels like a story.

Take a few minutes to tap through on your phone and note what works and what you'd change. We'll discuss as a class.

Save, commit, and push to GitHub. Before you push, remember to visit your repository's settings to activate GitHub Pages. Watch the Actions tab to confirm the deploy succeeds, then open your live site on your phone.

## Homework

### Task 1: Build your own photo story

Find a collection of public domain photographs from a digital archive and build your own full-screen photo essay. Good sources:

| Source | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| [Library of Congress](https://www.loc.gov/free-to-use/) | The nation's oldest federal cultural institution, with millions of photographs in the public domain |
| [NYPL Digital Collections](https://digitalcollections.nypl.org/) | New York Public Library's archive of maps, prints, photographs, and manuscripts |
| [Smithsonian Open Access](https://www.si.edu/openaccess) | The world's largest museum and research complex, with millions of CC0 images |
| [Wikimedia Commons](https://commons.wikimedia.org/) | A community-curated media repository with freely licensed and public domain files |
| [National Archives](https://www.archives.gov/research/catalog) | The US government's official repository of federal records and photographs |

Your project must have:

* A `TitleSlide` with a headline and introduction
* At least 8 `PhotoSlide` entries with caption overlays
* At least two `TextSlide` entries that provide editorial context
* A `CreditsSlide` with source information and proper attribution
* A design that works on mobile — test it on your phone before submitting

Think about the arc before you start building. What's your lede? What context does the reader need in the middle? How does it end?

Deploy it to GitHub Pages and send me the link.

### Task 2: Prepare to present

Be ready to share your photo story with the class. You should be prepared to:

* Show the story on your phone or in a narrow browser window
* Explain your editorial choices — which photos you selected, how you sequenced them, and what story you're telling
* Walk through how the `type` field in your JSON drives the structure of the page
* Discuss how you found and verified the public domain status of your images
